1
00:00:02,015 --> 00:00:10,931
Welcome to Italy's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:23,827
We work through 20 of the Italy regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:27,809
Let's bring Trentino-Alto Adige into view.

4
00:00:45,666 --> 00:00:56,289
Trentino-Alto Adige sits in the far north of Italy, a landlocked region
between Lombardia and Veneto, with Trento as its largest city in the upper
Adige valley.

5
00:00:56,968 --> 00:01:09,843
The name holds two places at once, the city of Trento and the upper valley of
the Adige river, and the Alto Adige half was Austrian until 1919, so most
people there still speak German.

6
00:01:10,513 --> 00:01:20,231
Remember the northern alpine corner that keeps most of its own taxes and half
of its German, with the Dolomites rising along its northern edge.

7
00:01:21,069 --> 00:01:24,865
Let's bring Friuli-Venezia Giulia into view.

8
00:01:42,334 --> 00:01:54,827
Friuli-Venezia Giulia lies in the north-east of Italy on the Adriatic coast,
with Veneto as its only Italian neighbour and Trieste as its largest city in
the far corner.

9
00:01:55,509 --> 00:02:08,048
The name uses Julius twice, because Friuli is the worn-down Latin Forum Iulii,
the market town of Julius Caesar, and Venezia Giulia was coined for these
lands in 1863.

10
00:02:08,701 --> 00:02:19,324
Remember the corner where the market town of Julius Caesar grew into a region,
with Trieste watching the Adriatic and the Alps at its back.

11
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:22,412
Let's bring Veneto into view.

12
00:02:41,529 --> 00:02:53,729
Veneto fills the north-east of Italy, from the Alps down to the Adriatic
coast, with Verona as its largest city and Friuli-Venezia Giulia on its
eastern side.

13
00:02:54,387 --> 00:03:03,129
It is the mainland that Venice ruled for four centuries, and it takes its name
from the Veneti, the people who were living here before Rome existed.

14
00:03:03,779 --> 00:03:15,726
Remember the mainland behind Venice, where Prosecco and Amarone come from and
the arena at Verona, standing since the year 30, still stages opera in the
open air.

15
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:19,213
Let's bring Valle d'Aosta into view.

16
00:03:26,088 --> 00:03:37,303
Valle d'Aosta is Italy's smallest region in the far north-west, a landlocked
valley with Piemonte as its only Italian neighbour and Aosta as its largest
town.

17
00:03:37,966 --> 00:03:51,016
It is the only region made of one valley, one river and one road with rock
walls either side, and Aosta was founded by the Emperor Augustus in 25 BC.

18
00:03:51,678 --> 00:04:00,629
Remember the tiniest region, a single road climbing a single valley towards
Mont Blanc, where French is spoken beside Italian.

19
00:04:01,363 --> 00:04:03,836
Let's bring Lombardia into view.

20
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:23,916
Lombardia spreads across the north of Italy, a landlocked region reaching from
the Alps down to the plain of the Po, with Milan as its largest city.

21
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:37,119
Its name comes from the Longobards, the long-beards, a Germanic people who
took northern Italy in 568, and in the Middle Ages a Lombard was simply a
moneylender.

22
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:45,152
Remember the region whose bankers lent their name to a street in London, and
which still makes about a fifth of Italy's economy.

23
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:48,235
Let's bring Piemonte into view.

24
00:04:57,126 --> 00:05:08,388
Piemonte lies in the north-west of Italy, a landlocked region with the Alps
wrapping it on three sides, and Turin as its largest city in the north-east
corner.

25
00:05:09,038 --> 00:05:21,019
The name means at the foot of the mountains, and this was the kingdom that
annexed the rest of Italy rather than joining it, with Turin as the first
capital of the unified country in 1861.

26
00:05:21,683 --> 00:05:31,110
Remember the kingdom that swallowed the rest of Italy, with vermouth, Nutella
and the white truffles of Alba all coming from this one region.

27
00:05:31,878 --> 00:05:35,419
Let's bring Emilia-Romagna into view.

28
00:05:42,945 --> 00:05:51,861
Emilia-Romagna runs across the north of Italy from the Alps to the Adriatic
coast, with Bologna as its largest city in the middle of the plain.

29
00:05:52,511 --> 00:06:06,025
The Via Aemilia, the road the Romans built in 187 BC, is still the straight
line through the region, and it is why almost every town here sits in a row
along it.

30
00:06:06,683 --> 00:06:16,447
Remember the straight Roman road with the food towns lined up beside it, where
Parmesan, Parma ham and balsamic vinegar all come from.

31
00:06:17,164 --> 00:06:19,637
Let's bring Liguria into view.

32
00:06:27,038 --> 00:06:38,427
Liguria is the narrow crescent of coast in the north-west of Italy, curving
round the Gulf of Genoa between Piemonte and Toscana, with Genoa as its
largest city.

33
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:48,670
The Ligurian Sea takes its name from the region rather than the other way
round, and the people called the Ligures were here before the Romans and
before the Celts.

34
00:06:49,332 --> 00:06:57,819
Remember the crescent coast of stacked fishing villages, where pesto was
invented in Genoa and the basil for it still grows in greenhouses.

35
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:00,748
Let's bring Toscana into view.

36
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:17,534
Toscana fills the centre of Italy, reaching from the Ligurian coast to the
Adriatic shore, with Florence as its largest city and Lazio to its south.

37
00:07:18,193 --> 00:07:30,175
Its name comes from the Tusci, the Roman name for the Etruscans, whose country
this was before Rome had one, and its dialect became the Italian language
because Dante and Petrarch wrote in it.

38
00:07:30,831 --> 00:07:40,897
Remember the region that gave Italy its own tongue, with the Leaning Tower of
Pisa and the hill towns of Tuscany between Florence and Siena.

39
00:07:41,739 --> 00:07:43,829
Let's bring Marche into view.

40
00:07:57,868 --> 00:08:08,317
Marche is the narrow strip of the Adriatic coast in the centre of Italy,
between Emilia-Romagna and Abruzzo, with Ancona as its largest city.

41
00:08:08,979 --> 00:08:18,743
The name is plural because a march was a frontier province of the Holy Roman
Empire, and this coast held more than one of them at once.

42
00:08:19,410 --> 00:08:28,280
Remember the Italian Marches, a shoreline of small white hill towns with more
theatres per head than any other region.

43
00:08:29,232 --> 00:08:31,740
Let's bring Umbria into view.

44
00:08:44,938 --> 00:08:55,817
Umbria sits in the centre of Italy, a landlocked region bordered only by
Toscana, Marche and Lazio, with Perugia as its largest city.

45
00:08:56,476 --> 00:09:05,776
It is the only region on the mainland with no coast and no foreign border,
touching nothing but other Italian regions, and St Francis was born at Assisi
here.

46
00:09:06,435 --> 00:09:14,829
Remember the green heart of Italy, the landlocked region of hill towns whose
basilica at Assisi was painted by Giotto.

47
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:18,046
Let's bring Abruzzo into view.

48
00:09:33,212 --> 00:09:44,172
Abruzzo runs down the Adriatic side of central Italy, between Marche and Lazio
and reaching south to Molise, with Pescara as its largest city.

49
00:09:44,826 --> 00:09:54,764
A third of the region is national park, the highest and wildest stretch of the
Apennines, and it is the last place in Italy where wild bears still live.

50
00:09:55,422 --> 00:10:03,991
Remember the mountains that drop straight into the sea, with the Corno Grande
above the park holding the southernmost glacier in Europe, now nearly gone.

51
00:10:04,823 --> 00:10:06,866
Let's bring Lazio into view.

52
00:10:21,252 --> 00:10:31,062
Lazio fills the centre of Italy, reaching from the Tyrrhenian coast inland to
the Apennines, with Rome as its largest city on the Tiber.

53
00:10:31,727 --> 00:10:43,453
Latium is the land of the Latins, whose village on the Tiber became Rome and
whose language became Latin, and the region is still named after the tribe
rather than the city.

54
00:10:44,125 --> 00:10:52,101
Remember the land of the Latins, which holds two sovereign states inside Rome,
the Vatican and the headquarters of the Order of Malta.

55
00:10:52,830 --> 00:10:55,593
Let's bring Molise into view.

56
00:11:05,634 --> 00:11:16,931
Molise is a tiny region on the Adriatic coast of central Italy, with Abruzzo
above it and Puglia below, and Campobasso as its largest city.

57
00:11:17,593 --> 00:11:26,544
It is Italy's youngest region, split away from Abruzzo in 1963, and the second
smallest after Valle d'Aosta.

58
00:11:27,239 --> 00:11:37,305
Remember the region so little known that Italians joke that Molise does not
exist, with its own short stretch of Adriatic sand.

59
00:11:38,140 --> 00:11:40,311
Let's bring Puglia into view.

60
00:11:56,537 --> 00:12:07,880
Puglia is the heel of the boot in the far south-east of Italy, facing the
Adriatic Sea on one side and the Ionian Sea on the other, with Bari as its
largest city.

61
00:12:08,545 --> 00:12:19,714
Its name comes from Apulia, the Roman name for the land of the Apuli, and it
grows about forty per cent of Italy's olive oil from trees that are often
centuries old.

62
00:12:20,368 --> 00:12:30,817
Remember the heel of the boot, with the white cone-roofed trulli of
Alberobello and old olive trees on the plains behind Bari.

63
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:34,342
Let's bring Campania into view.

64
00:12:52,483 --> 00:13:01,910
Campania runs along the Tyrrhenian Sea in the south of Italy, from the border
with Lazio round to Basilicata and Puglia, with Naples as its largest city.

65
00:13:02,576 --> 00:13:12,851
The Romans named it for its plain, the most fertile in Italy, and called it
the happy countryside, because Vesuvius keeps covering the fields in ash.

66
00:13:13,514 --> 00:13:26,645
Remember the happy countryside of Naples, where the same volcano that made the
soil rich buried Pompeii, and where pizza margherita was named for a queen in
1889.

67
00:13:27,518 --> 00:13:29,770
Let's bring Basilicata into view.

68
00:13:47,345 --> 00:14:00,345
Basilicata is the mountainous region in the south-east of Italy, between
Campania and Puglia with a short stretch of Ionian coast, and Potenza as its
largest city.

69
00:14:01,011 --> 00:14:13,631
The name comes from a Greek word meaning the emperor's man, the Byzantine
governor who ran it from Constantinople, and it has also been called Lucania
after the people who were there first.

70
00:14:14,291 --> 00:14:21,756
Remember the mountains of the emperor's man, and the cave dwellings of Matera
that people have lived in for nine thousand years.

71
00:14:22,559 --> 00:14:25,322
Let's bring Sardegna into view.

72
00:14:33,861 --> 00:14:46,354
Sardegna is the big island off the west of Italy, south of Corsica and
separated from the mainland by the Tyrrhenian Sea, with Cagliari as its
largest city.

73
00:14:47,026 --> 00:14:54,654
Its nuraghi, some seven thousand dry-stone towers and some older than Rome
itself, exist nowhere else on earth.

74
00:14:55,306 --> 00:15:04,478
Remember the island of stone towers that ringed the Bronze Age villages, and
the blue zone where islanders live past a hundred more often than anywhere
else.

75
00:15:05,328 --> 00:15:07,290
Let's bring Calabria into view.

76
00:15:20,369 --> 00:15:30,864
Calabria is the toe of the boot in the far south-east of Italy, across the
strait from Sicily, with Reggio di Calabria as its largest city and Basilicata
to the north.

77
00:15:31,517 --> 00:15:40,259
The name once belonged to the heel and not to the toe, because Roman Calabria
was the Salento, and the name slid across the gulf in the Byzantine centuries.

78
00:15:40,927 --> 00:15:50,517
Remember the toe that took the heel's name, with the Riace bronzes pulled from
the sea off its coast by a snorkeller in 1972.

79
00:15:51,314 --> 00:15:53,439
Let's bring Sicilia into view.

80
00:16:08,010 --> 00:16:17,983
Sicilia is the great island in the south of Italy, the largest island in the
Mediterranean, with Palermo as its largest city on the north shore.

81
00:16:18,636 --> 00:16:28,574
Its name comes from the Sicels, one of three peoples on the island when the
Greeks arrived, the others being the Sicani and the Elymians.

82
00:16:29,235 --> 00:16:37,594
Remember Etna, the tallest active volcano in Europe, whose ash keeps the
slopes below it farmed and the vineyards alive.

83
00:16:51,489 --> 00:17:20,253
20 regions of Italy, one for each target of the map: Trentino-Alto Adige,
Friuli-Venezia Giulia, Veneto, Valle d'Aosta, Lombardia, Piemonte,
Emilia-Romagna, Liguria, Toscana, Marche, Umbria, Abruzzo, Lazio, Molise,
Puglia, Campania, Basilicata, Sardegna, Calabria and Sicilia.

84
00:17:20,917 --> 00:17:30,635
Remember the shape of Italy from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
